# DRAFT: bounded provider-backed development

Status: preparatory proposal only. This is not an approved implementation plan, permission to call a model, or a completed capability. Current runtime-04 source/configuration/image and campaign history remain unchanged. No remote calls are authorized.

## Recommendation and alternatives

Reuse the existing ledger-backed OpenRouter one-shot gateway under explicit `development_sp500_v2` provider authority. Preserve the provisional S&P data designation, `disabled_development` semantics, policy-only workspace export, exact source/data/evaluator identities, existing local resource ownership and cleanup, and provider-free controller compatibility. Grant neither apply nor held-out permissions. Full production universes are unnecessary for this explicitly provisional mode, but current code intentionally forbids it; configuration alone cannot enable it.

Waiting for production data avoids a new supported combination but postpones the real model-to-evaluation development loop and conflates model wiring with universe completion. An ad-hoc bridge that fetches responses and imports them as controller responses is shorter superficially but loses the existing paid-call authority, budget, uncertainty, and history bindings. Do not adopt that bridge. Integrate the existing gateway at the shared invoker seam without duplicating evaluation infrastructure.

## Source-backed change map

References below are repository-relative source paths and current line numbers, which may shift during implementation.

- `core/pit_optimizer_v5/contracts.py:345,1087`: ProviderCapabilities already binds model, role-call/total-token/per-role-output ceilings, optional USD ceiling requiring a matching price upper bound when selected, input overhead, retries and repair policy. CampaignManifest currently prohibits any provider for development. Replace that blanket prohibition with explicit validation of the supported combination; preserve scope and semantic restrictions and production behavior. Do not add permissive defaults.
- `core/pit_optimizer_v5/manifest.py:617`: the manifest builder independently prohibits a development provider. Update this guard with the same explicit supported combination as CampaignManifestV5; changing the dataclass and preparation path alone is insufficient. Preserve explicit scope/semantic pairing and authenticated manifest construction.
- `core/pit_optimizer_v5/development_preparation.py:33,46,82,145,190`: split development data/semantic validation from controller-only validation. Existing config requires response_directory and contains no gateway/ledger identity. Prefer an explicitly distinct provider-development config type with the existing immutable host identities plus gateway/ledger identities, credential handle, and policy-only export. Preserve decoding and hashes of old configs; no migration/reinterpretation of live config. Composition/factory validation must require the correct exact invoker graph for each mode, authenticated manifest and baseline, owner, and export mode.
- `development_preparation.py:255,279,301,319,607`: controller request/response and package authentication must remain controller-only. Extend baseline import/setup to bind explicit provider capabilities for a new campaign, rather than replacing provider=None in existing artifacts. Shared development history must dispatch on authenticated provider authority: controller packages for provider-free, exact ledger-backed packages for provider-backed. Neither provenance may substitute for the other.
- `core/pit_optimizer_v5/cli.py:391,544,590,831,964,1031`: retain production standalone restrictions. Factor/reuse gateway construction from the production wrapper; inject its ledger-backed invoker into the existing shared round composition with policy-only export. Reuse exact gateway/ledger graph checks rather than accepting an arbitrary invoker. Config loading and development verify/summarize entry paths must recognize the explicit provider-development config. The existing provider-present branch of local run verification already uses paid ledger authentication; preserve its exact request/package/resource/projection checks and select correct export mode.
- `core/pit_optimizer_v5/operations.py:128,148,196,277,289,479,499,528,639`: derive gateway identities according to provider mode, not production data scope. Preparation must remain call-free and render a concrete launch command. Select correct factory/config and round path fields; provider-backed development must not require response_directory. Crucially, the current started-with-no-journal recovery exemption is based on DevelopmentAdapterConfig type: restrict that exemption to controller-only authority. Paid uncertainty must retain durable reservation/receipt recovery and must never silently restart a round or refresh deadlines. Reuse existing shared campaign budget stopping logic.
- `core/pit_optimizer_v5/runtime.py:979,1144,1180`: paid dependency validation already expects LedgerBackedRoleInvoker. Terminal-authority selection currently forces development to controller provenance; split by explicit provider authority. Preserve controller pending behavior only for provider-free mode, ledger completion/recovery for provider-backed mode, and fixture authority only where already allowed. Do not manufacture a controller receipt for a paid completion.
- `core/pit_optimizer_v5/summary.py:229,291`: development history currently passes through the provider-free requirement; update authenticated mode dispatch with the helper above. Keep provider usage reporting and typed failures truthful, including unknown accepted calls, while preserving existing failed controller campaign summaries.
- `core/pit_optimizer_v5/production_provider.py:66,165,215,257,515,570`: reuse LocalRoleAuthorizationLedger, OpenRouterOneShotJsonCompletion, reservation and completion accounting. Ledger construction already requires provider authority rather than production universe scope. Keep exact model matching, request identity, one-shot invocation, no automatic retries/repair, cost reservation when a monetary ceiling is selected, audited provider cost otherwise, and conservative unknown-outcome treatment. No new transport is proposed.
- `core/pit_optimizer_v5/provider.py:923,1031,1219,1478,1914,3427`: reuse closed role inputs, request schemas and wire serialization. Investigator receives aggregate evidence/archive/critic/history; author receives authenticated editable policy source, hypothesis and policy contracts; critic receives evaluation aggregates, predictions, typed failures and explicitly unavailable semantics. This includes source disclosure and must be stated plainly in authorization.

During implementation, perform a scoped source search for remaining development/provider=None assumptions in these modules and their direct authority consumers. This is a requirement to verify the map, not permission for an unrelated refactor.

## Phases and permitted verification

1. Review this draft and settle the support boundary. Keep runtime-04 untouched until terminal cleanup. No tests are to be read, created or run; no synthetic probes.
2. Implement the explicit authority/config/invoker split and recovery/history dispatch above. Preserve old artifacts and provider-free behavior. Use source review, scoped Ruff, in-memory compilation and CLI help/rendering only; no provider invocation or credential reads.
3. Independently review composition, paid uncertainty handling, provenance separation and outbound schema. Exercise authorized local validate-only preparation/rendering against actual inputs, with no gateway call. Verify identities and caps in the resulting review package.
4. Contracts and runtime are evaluator-closure files. Rebuild/reseal using the existing authorization for local evaluator work and the required elevated execution path, create the new digest-pinned profile, and obtain new authentic parent baseline reports against the same provisional panel/data selection. Do not copy old reports across evaluator identities or rerun synthetic evidence. Any incidental image-covered changes require the same treatment.
5. Prepare a separately named, create-only campaign and exact launch/resume commands. Present the package and unresolved choices below for remote-call approval. Only after explicit approval run a bounded real campaign; retain all attempts, original starts, receipts, failures, checkpoints and cleanup. No apply or held-out evaluation follows automatically.

## Review package and unresolved authorization choices

Do not choose a model or price from memory. Required decisions are exact OpenRouter model ID and acceptable routing/provider disclosure; total token ceiling; per-role output ceiling; input-token overhead bound; optional USD ceiling versus audited-cost-only mode (historical plans include an explicit audit-only preference); model input/output price upper bounds and provenance if a USD ceiling is selected; round/call/time/resource limits; and permitted policy-source disclosure. One existing capability applies a single model and output cap across roles: differing models/caps by role would expand scope and are not recommended for this increment.

Suggested shape for review, not authorization: at most two rounds and six calls total (one investigator, author and critic each round), zero automatic retries and schema-repair calls. Finite call, token and wall-time bounds are required. Their values and the monetary policy remain unresolved until deliberately supplied; audit-only USD is an existing supported choice, not a missing safety capability. Do not invent a user budget or make a USD ceiling mandatory. Never describe six calls as guaranteed completion: preflight input/cost reservation or failures can stop earlier.

Create an inspectable bundle containing the manifest/capabilities, matching price authority when a USD ceiling is selected, baseline and plan refs, data/source/evaluator/profile digests, explicit provisional/semantic/export permissions, adapter config/gateway/ledger identities, owner, exact launch command, and cleanup/deadline bounds. Include exact serialized initial outbound messages and JSON schemas with hashes and conservative input/output bounds, plus cost bounds if the selected monetary policy requires them. Later author/critic packets depend on real preceding outputs: provide their closed field/source envelope and generation rules now, then durably bind actual packets before each call. Clearly disclose editable policy source and aggregate strategy performance/history; raw prices, universe identifiers, credentials, environment and full repository are outside the proposed role payload envelope. Validate the actual bytes rather than relying on a prose promise.

Credential reference is the handle `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` only. Do not display, read for this proposal, or package its value. No purchase or external transmission is authorized by this document. Approval must name the model, destination, caps and outgoing scope; changing them requires new authority, not an override of the existing campaign.

## Independent proposal review

The reviewer identified the separate manifest-builder prohibition at manifest.py:617; the change map now includes it. The remaining scope was accepted as bounded reuse with separate controller/paid provenance and optional USD accounting. This was a static proposal review, not implementation or execution evidence. Current campaign source remains unchanged.
